  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a producer?",
      "a": "A producer is a living thing that makes its own food by photosynthesis, usually a green plant or alga. Every food chain starts with a producer.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the grass"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a primary consumer?",
      "a": "A primary consumer is an animal that eats producers. Zebras, elephants and grasshoppers that eat plants are primary consumers, also called herbivores.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the zebra"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What would happen if the zebras disappeared?",
      "a": "Lions and vultures would lose a major food source and might decline or switch to other prey, while grass might grow taller. Because everything is linked, one change spreads through the web.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the lion"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do decomposers do?",
      "a": "Decomposers, such as bacteria, fungi and termites, break down dead plants, animals and dung, returning minerals to the soil for producers to use again.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the termites"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between a food chain and a food web?",
      "a": "A food chain shows one line of feeding, like grass to zebra to lion. A food web joins many food chains together, showing that most animals eat, and are eaten by, more than one thing.",
      "asks": [
        "Food chain vs food web"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In food chains and webs, arrows point from the food to the organism that eats it, showing the direction energy flows.",
    "The Serengeti in Tanzania is home to over a million wildebeest and hundreds of thousands of zebras in its yearly migration."
  ],
